.TH J0 3 2026-05-11 "Substrate LibM" "Substrate Library Functions"
.SH NAME
j0, j1, jn, y0, y1, yn,
j0f, j1f, jnf, y0f, y1f, ynf,
j0l, j1l, jnl, y0l, y1l, ynl
\- Bessel functions of the first and second kinds
.SH LIBRARY
Math library
.RB ( libm ", " -lm )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.sp
.BI "double j0(double " x ");"
.br
.BI "double j1(double " x ");"
.br
.BI "double jn(int " n ", double " x ");"
.sp
.BI "double y0(double " x ");"
.br
.BI "double y1(double " x ");"
.br
.BI "double yn(int " n ", double " x ");"
.sp
.BI "float  j0f(float " x ");"
.br
.BI "float  j1f(float " x ");"
.br
.BI "float  jnf(int " n ", float " x ");"
.br
.BI "float  y0f(float " x ");"
.br
.BI "float  y1f(float " x ");"
.br
.BI "float  ynf(int " n ", float " x ");"
.sp
.BI "long double j0l(long double " x ");"
.br
.BI "long double j1l(long double " x ");"
.br
.BI "long double jnl(int " n ", long double " x ");"
.br
.BI "long double y0l(long double " x ");"
.br
.BI "long double y1l(long double " x ");"
.br
.BI "long double ynl(int " n ", long double " x ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR j0 ()
and
.BR j1 ()
functions return Bessel functions of
.IR x ,
of the first kind, of orders 0 and 1 respectively;
.BR jn ()
returns the Bessel function of
.I x
of the first kind of order
.IR n .
.PP
The
.BR y0 ()
and
.BR y1 ()
functions return Bessel functions of
.IR x ,
of the second kind, of orders 0 and 1 respectively;
.BR yn ()
returns the Bessel function of
.I x
of the second kind of order
.IR n .
.PP
The
.B f
and
.B l
suffixed variants take and return
.BR float " and " "long double" ,
respectively.  They are computed by delegating to the
.BR double
implementation; substrate's
.BR long " " double
is i386 x87 80-bit extended precision but the Bessel routines work in
.BR double
internally.
.SH ALGORITHM
For |x| up to 8, the implementation uses the Taylor series expansion
in z = (x/2)².  For |x| > 8, it switches to the standard Hankel
asymptotic expansion via the auxiliary P_n / Q_n modulus and phase
functions.  For
.BR jn ()
with
.I n
≥ 2, forward recurrence is used when n ≤ x (stable) and Miller's
backward recurrence with the standard normalisation J_0 + 2(J_2 + J_4
+ ...) = 1 is used otherwise (forward recurrence is numerically
unstable in that regime).
.BR yn ()
uses forward recurrence throughout, which is always stable for Y.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The
.BR j0 (), j1 (), " and " jn ()
functions return the Bessel value of
.IR x .
For finite
.IR x ,
the value is in [-1, 1] for J_0 and small for higher orders.
.PP
The
.BR y0 (), y1 (), " and " yn ()
functions return the Bessel value of
.IR x ,
which diverges to
.BR -inf
as
.I x
→ 0⁺.
.PP
If
.I x
is NaN, all functions return NaN with no exception.
.PP
If
.I x
is +inf, all functions return 0.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.BR EDOM " (y-family only)"
The argument
.I x
is negative.  The function returns NaN and raises
.BR FE_INVALID .
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    /* J_0 has its first zero near 2.40483. */
    printf("j0(2.40483) = %g\\n", j0(2.40483));

    /* Three-term recurrence:
     *   J_{n-1}(x) + J_{n+1}(x) = (2n/x) J_n(x).
     */
    double x = 3.0;
    int n = 4;
    double lhs = jn(n - 1, x) + jn(n + 1, x);
    double rhs = (2.0 * n / x) * jn(n, x);
    printf("recurrence error = %g\\n", lhs - rhs);

    /* Y is unbounded at zero. */
    printf("y0(1e-10) = %g\\n", y0(1e-10));

    return 0;
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sqrt (3),
.BR sin (3),
.BR cos (3),
.BR log (3),
.BR feraiseexcept (3)
.SH STANDARDS
The double-precision routines
.BR j0 (), j1 (), jn (), y0 (), y1 (), " and " yn ()
are XSI / POSIX.1-2024 extensions to ISO C.  The
.B f
and
.B l
suffixed variants are BSD / glibc extensions and follow the same
naming convention as the rest of substrate's
.BR libm .
.SH HISTORY
.BR j0 ()
through
.BR yn ()
appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX as a fixture of every Unix
math library since.
